WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.080
Responsible threat actor profiles use confidence levels and analytic uncertainty.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.020
A high-confidence statement should rest on strong, relevant, and corroborated evidence.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.920
A low-confidence statement may be useful, but it should be clearly labeled.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:22.380
Corroboration matters, but analysts must watch for circular reporting, where multiple sources repeat the same original claim without independent verification.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.480
More citations do not always mean more evidence.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.340
Bias control is part of analytic quality.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:35.520
Confirmation bias leads analysts to favor evidence that supports an existing belief while discounting contrary information.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.380
Recency bias can overweight the newest report.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.720
Mirror imaging can cause analysts to assume an actor thinks or operates like the defender would.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:50.820
Naming collisions, actor rebranding, overlapping clusters, and inconsistent public labels can all create confusion.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.240
AI can amplify these issues if the prompt rewards confident summaries over careful reasoning.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.060
Attribution has limits.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:06.360
A profile may discuss a named group, an unattributed cluster, a campaign, a toolset, or a behavior pattern, but it should not overclaim.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:13.220
Some profiles are most useful when they avoid attribution entirely and focus on what defenders can observe and prepare for.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:22.320
When attribution is included, the report should separate facts, judgments, hypotheses, assumptions, alternatives, confidence, and unknowns.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.740
Structured analytic language makes reporting more defensible.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:32.200
Words such as likely, possible, assessed, confirmed, and unknown should be used consistently.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.020
The profile should explain what would change the judgment and what evidence is missing.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:45.200
AI can help draft the report, but human analysts must validate sources, manage uncertainty, avoid bias, and report responsibly.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.740
The audience should leave with clearer defensive decisions, not inflated certainty.
